sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A flattened cell shaped like a quadrant of a circle divides into equal halves by a circular-arc anticlinal wall meeting the side and periphery at right angles, cutting off about 55° 22′ of the arc and starting about three-tenths of the radius from the centre, since this is the shortest bisecting wall.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A quadrant-shaped flat cell divides by a curved anticlinal wall about 3:7 along its side";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "bisecting a quadrant: circular arc \\perp side and periphery, cutting off 55^\\circ 22' of the arc, starting \\approx 0.3r from the centre";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Division of a flattened quadrant cell”. Scope: flattened discoid algae (e.g. Erythrotrichia) and eggs flattened under pressure (frog, sea-urchin) at the eight-cell stage"
}
